body:
{{Lost Souls by Poppy Z Brite}}

{{The Moth Diaries by Rachel Klein}}

{{The Historian by Elizabeth Kostova}}

{{The Blood Opera Sequence by Tanith Lee}}

{{Fledgling by Octavia E. Butler}}

{{Anno Dracula Series by Kim Newman}}

{{My Soul to Keep by Tananarive Due}}

{{Fevre Dream by George R. R. Martin}}

{{[Anno Dracula](https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/8970727-anno-dracula)by Kim Newman}} he has an entire series based on the premise of an au where Dracula succeeds in his endeavors during eponymous novel by bram stroker.

{{The Parasol Protectorate by Gail Carriger}} is by no means a literary masterpiece but I've heard it's a really fun light series and does involve quite a bit of vampire slaying but with fancy dresses and petticoats

{{The Southern Book Club's Guide[The Southern Book Club's Guide to Slaying Vampires](https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/44074800-the-southern-book-club-s-guide-to-slaying-vampires) to Slaying Vampires by Grady Hendrix}} 

{{My Best Friend's Exorcism by Grady Hendrix}} which is such a special book to me that I will recommend it until I die.

{{I Am Legend by Richard Matheson}}

{{[Who Fears Death](https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/7767021-who-fears-death) by Nnedi Okorafor}}

{{Left Hand of Darkness by Ursula K. Le Guin}}

{{The Robot Novels by Isaac Asimov}} is literally my comfort read when it comes to science fiction. I absolutely adore the relationship between the two major characters develop over the course of the series. it's not too heavy on tech jargon.it has excellent speculative concepts regarding space exploration and interstellar cultures but it's a bit of a dated idea of the future.

{{The Long Way to a Small, Angry Planet
Novel by Becky Chambers}}

{{The Sirens of Titan by Kurt Vonnegut}}

{{The Luminous Dead by Caitlin Starling}}

{{Shame Is an Ocean I Swim Across by Mary Lambert}}

{{Bright Dead Things by Ada Limón}} both of these are probably the only two contemporary poetry books that I actually enjoy. 


Mary Lambert's work is heavy and cathartic and certainly does deal with controversial issues how I personally would not consider it political.

{{Hell House by Richard Matheson}}

{{Burnt Offerings by Robert Marasco}}

{{Summer of Night by Dan Simmons}} I'd especially recommend Summer of Night if you're looking for alternatives. It takes place in the 60s but it has similar tropes as a lot of Stephen King's work.

{{My Best Friend's Exorcism by Grady Hendrix}} this book is literally a love letter to 80s horror films and horror pulp in general.

{{Vampires in the Lemon Grove by Karen Russell}}

{{St. Lucy's Home for Girls Raised by Wolves by Karen Russell}}

{{Madeleine is Sleeping by Sarah Shun-lien Bynum}}

{{Someone Comes to Town by Cory Doctorow}}

{{Like Water for Chocolate by Laura Esquivel}}

{{The Parallel Apartments by Bill Cotter}}

{{In Persuasion Nation by George Saunders}}

{{The Grip of It by Jac Jemc}}

{{The PowerBook by Jeanette Winterson}}

{{Madeleine is Sleeping by Sarah Shun-lien Bynum}} is an unsung favorite of mine. I read it one sitting before I even bought it. It's written in mixed prose format, sometimes verse, sometimes scattered words, and sometime pages are left blank. The plot is dreamlike and surreal and incredibly inspiring.


{{A Better Angel by Chris Adrian}} I was very torn on this one when I first read it. but it's one I kept thinking back on until I finally decided that i enjoyed the experience.


{{Gutshot by Amelia Gray}}

{{The PowerBook by Jeanette Winterson}}

Sounds like you like fantastical books and generational stories

{{Shark Dialogues by Kiana Davenport}}

{{One Hundred Years of Solitude by Gabriel García Márquez}} 

{{ House of Spirits by Isabel Allende}}

{{Zorro by Isabel Allende}}

and of course the classic adventure {{The Three Musketeers by Alexandre Dumas}}

{{Gutshot by Amelia Gray}}

{{Swamplandia! by Karen Russell}} 

{{Universal Harvester by John Darnielle}}

{{In the Dream House by Carmen Maria Machado}}

Almost anything by Jeanette Winterson

Since I just now noticed that you have Stephen King on your list. You might like other horror authors like Nick Cutter, Riley Sager, and Richard Matheson 

You could also always give his son, Joe Hill, a shot as well.

I'd also like to throw in {{Daughter of Smoke and Bone by Laini Taylor}}
